{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#994","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#994","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#994\",\"parent_revision\":\"da3747ad957987df67bb67d06d56c6d1265f586b3afc8268bf381c2887dc7057\",\"root_revision\":\"da3747ad957987df67bb67d06d56c6d1265f586b3afc8268bf381c2887dc7057\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nTask completion is trust-based. The SubagentStop hook marks tasks complete when the sub-agent exits, but does not verify the agent's output. Observed in vm-flightsimulator: T06 returned \\\"Now I'll delegate...\\\" (did nothing) and was marked complete. T04 returned \\\"line 347 still needs fixing\\\" (incomplete) and was marked complete. Both caught only by manual grep/glob verification.\\n\\nA PostToolUse hook on SubagentStop should read the agent's output and check for completion signals (file exists, tests pass, no \\\"still needs fixing\\\" language) before marking the task complete. The vm-pilot-inspector pattern (dispatch an inspector after each agent completes) is a reference implementation.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **systematic agent output verification before marking tasks complete** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent feedback \u2014 vm-flightsimulator session 2026-03-22, suggestion #2\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-22)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n- Goal: Systematically verify agent output before the SubagentStop hook marks a SAM task complete, replacing trust-based completion with mechanical output inspection\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE: task_status_hook.py SubagentStop handler, run_strict_pre_completion_checks stub, CLAUDE_SKILLS_HOOK_PROFILE=strict env var control, SamTask.acceptance_criteria field, related groomed item #1857, agent transcript path in hook_input\\n- DERIVABLE: T06 and T04 failure modes can be modeled as detectable output-signal patterns in JSONL transcript; vm-pilot-inspector behavior derivable from description and existing improvement-generator agent\\n- MISSING: Scope boundary between #994 (transcript output-signal scan) and #1857 (AC checklist evaluation) undefined. Output-signal vocabulary not formally defined \u2014 only two examples exist. vm-pilot-inspector pattern not implemented in codebase.\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Feature (hook behavior change + optional new agent)\\n- Behavioral boundary: The SubagentStop hook's decision to mark a SAM task COMPLETE \u2014 specifically, whether it does so based solely on the agent exiting, or whether it first reads and validates the agent's output from the JSONL transcript\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that a task-worker that produced provably incomplete or delegating-only output is NOT marked COMPLETE by the hook, whereas a task-worker that produced substantive work output IS marked COMPLETE\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Create a SAM plan with two tasks and a `task-worker` sub-agent dispatched for each.\\n2. For task T06: write a task-worker prompt that causes the agent to respond only with a delegation promise (\\\"Now I'll delegate...\\\") and exit without writing any file or running any command.\\n3. For task T04: write a task-worker prompt that causes the agent to respond with \\\"line 347 still needs fixing\\\" and exit.\\n4. Observe: both tasks are marked COMPLETE by the SubagentStop hook.\\n5. Verify: `sam_task(plan=P, task=T06, action='read').status == 'complete'` and `sam_task(plan=P, task=T04, action='read').status == 'complete'`.\\n6. Grep the agent JSONL transcript for the actual assistant output \u2014 confirm it contains the delegation/incomplete signal.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- vm-flightsimulator session 2026-03-22: T06 last output was \\\"Now I'll delegate...\\\" (did nothing); T04 last output included \\\"line 347 still needs fixing\\\"\\n- Both were caught only by manual `grep`/`glob` verification after the hook had already marked them complete\\n- `task_status_hook.py` line 976: `_call_sam_task_state(plan_addr, task_id, SamTaskStatus.COMPLETE)` \u2014 no output-signal gate precedes this call\\n- `run_strict_pre_completion_checks` (lines 148\u2013173): checks `status != NOT_STARTED` and `acceptance_criteria != empty` \u2014 does not read the transcript's assistant output\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 Trust-based completion is a recurring silent-failure mode. Two concrete examples are documented. However, the adjacent item #1857 already addresses AC-validation at a higher semantic level. This item adds a lower-level transcript-signal layer. Planning must resolve whether both are needed or if #1857 supersedes this item.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Any pipeline that depends on SAM task status as a quality gate (complete-implementation, contract-verification, downstream task dependencies)\\n- Bottleneck: Silent incomplete tasks propagate as complete through the plan, requiring manual discovery via grep/glob at review time rather than at task boundary\\n- Affected agents: task-worker, any agent dispatched via the SubagentStop hook path with a `task-worker` matcher\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Catches \\\"delegation promise without execution\\\" pattern (T06 class) at task boundary instead of at manual review\\n- Catches \\\"reported incomplete work\\\" pattern (T04 class) at task boundary\\n- If implemented as a continuous-verification variant (inspector agent dispatched after each task), failures are caught while context is fresh and the fix cost is low\\n- Reduces the manual verification burden documented in the additional evidence section\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a task-worker sub-agent exits, the SubagentStop hook reads the agent's final output from the JSONL transcript. Before marking the task COMPLETE, it checks whether the output contains signals of incomplete or delegating-only work. If a negative signal is detected (e.g., \\\"Now I'll delegate\\\", \\\"still needs fixing\\\", \\\"I'll do X next time\\\"), the task is NOT marked COMPLETE \u2014 it is instead marked BLOCKED or FAILED, and a warning is emitted to stderr. If no negative signal is detected, the hook proceeds to mark the task COMPLETE as before.\\n\\nAlternatively (continuous-verification variant from the additional evidence): after SubagentStop, an inspector sub-agent is dispatched to review the completed agent's transcript and output artifacts before the status is finalized. The inspector emits a PASS/FAIL verdict that the hook acts on.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nOne of two target states (scope question for human):\\n\\n**Option A \u2014 Hook-level output-signal scanning** (lower overhead):\\n- `task_status_hook.py`: `handle_subagent_stop` calls a new `check_output_signals(transcript_path)` function before `_call_sam_task_state(..., COMPLETE)`\\n- `check_output_signals` reads the last N lines of the agent transcript, extracts the final assistant message text, and matches against a configurable pattern list\\n- On match, marks the task FAILED/BLOCKED instead of COMPLETE and logs the matched signal\\n- Controlled by `CLAUDE_SKILLS_HOOK_PROFILE=strict` or a new `CLAUDE_SKILLS_OUTPUT_VERIFICATION=true` variable\\n\\n**Option B \u2014 Post-task inspector agent** (higher fidelity):\\n- After SubagentStop, the hook (or an orchestrator listener) dispatches a lightweight inspector agent\\n- The inspector reads the transcript and output artifacts, emits a structured verdict\\n- The hook uses the verdict to determine final task status\\n- This is the \\\"vm-pilot-inspector\\\" pattern described in the item; no such agent exists in the codebase yet\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given a task-worker transcript whose final assistant message matches a known incomplete-output pattern (\\\"Now I'll delegate\\\", \\\"still needs fixing\\\", \\\"I'll fix this later\\\"), the SubagentStop hook does NOT call `_call_sam_task_state(..., COMPLETE)` for that task.\\n2. Given a task-worker transcript whose final assistant message contains a valid STATUS: DONE block or no incomplete-output signals, the hook marks the task COMPLETE as before.\\n3. Given `CLAUDE_SKILLS_HOOK_PROFILE=standard` (the default), the new verification behavior does NOT activate \u2014 existing sessions are unaffected.\\n4. Given a controlled test matching the T06 scenario (agent exits after saying \\\"Now I'll delegate...\\\"), `test_task_status_hook.py` demonstrates the task is marked BLOCKED/FAILED rather than COMPLETE.\\n5. Given a controlled test matching the T04 scenario (agent exits after saying \\\"line 347 still needs fixing\\\"), the same test demonstrates BLOCKED/FAILED status.\\n6. Given a task correctly completed with a commit and file evidence, the hook still marks the task COMPLETE within the hook's 60-second timeout budget.\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- Is #994 intended to complement #1857 (hook-level signal detection + AC semantic validation as two independent layers), or does #1857 subsume #994?\\n- Which option is preferred: A (hook-level transcript scan, low overhead) or B (post-task inspector agent, higher fidelity but adds a sub-agent dispatch)?\\n- What is the minimum viable signal vocabulary? The item names two patterns (delegation promise, \\\"still needs fixing\\\") \u2014 are there others from the vm-flightsimulator session that should be included?\\n- Should detection be activated by default (standard profile) or opt-in via strict profile or new env var?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Script | plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py |\\n| Config | plugins/development-harness/hooks/hooks.json |\\n| Skill | plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/SKILL.md |\\n| Skill | plugins/development-harness/skills/verify-done/SKILL.md |\\n| Agent | plugins/development-harness/agents/task-worker.md |\\n| Agent | plugins/development-harness/agents/tn-verification-gate.md |\\n| Backlog | #1857 \u2014 Add acceptance-criteria validation gate before task-worker marks complete (groomed, P1) |\\n| Tests | plugins/development-harness/tests/test_task_status_hook.py |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: Scope disambiguation vs. #1857 (whether #994 is additive or overlapping must be decided before implementation planning)\\n- Blocks: None identified\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Scope boundary between #994 (transcript output-signal scan) and #1857 (AC checklist evaluation) is undefined \u2014 planning cannot proceed until the human decides whether these are complementary layers or one supersedes the other\\n- The \\\"vm-pilot-inspector\\\" reference pattern is not implemented in this repository; if Option B is chosen, a new inspector agent must be designed from scratch\\n- The output-signal vocabulary (what patterns constitute incomplete output) is not formally defined; two examples exist but no exhaustive list\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium (Option A) / High (Option B) \u2014 Option A modifies an existing Python hook script with a new function, adds test cases for the two known failure scenarios, and adds an environment-variable toggle. Option B requires designing a new inspector agent, wiring it into the SubagentStop dispatch path, and handling the timing/timeout implications of dispatching a sub-agent from within a hook.\\n\\n### Additional Evidence\\n\\n**Also from claude_skills DH session (2026-03-22, \\\"more compute\\\" patterns)**: Instead of quality gates only at the end, run a verification agent after every task completion. Each task-worker's output gets a mini-review before the next wave starts. Catches issues early, when the context is fresh and the fix is cheap. This is the \\\"continuous verification\\\" variant of the same problem \u2014 trust-based completion without mechanical verification.\\n\\n## Work Brief Extension Required Outputs And Report Fidelity 2026 09 24\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-24T04:31:05.993644Z</sub>\\n\\n## Desired outcome\\n\\nThe planner, worker, and judge must share one observable completion contract for required state outputs. Each expected output is a required pair of `name` and stored-location `reference`. A worker must persist every listed output, read it back, compare the persisted report with the actions, observations, outputs, errors, and validation that actually occurred, and record the reference and result. The judge must independently read and verify each required output before acceptance.\\n\\nFailed, blocked, and needs-input results must identify every absent output and its reason without creating placeholders. CLEAR/CoVe task-result validation remains separate from report-fidelity checking. Every DH role must apply #3423's shared contract: transport status remains `DONE|BLOCKED`, durable result remains `complete|failed|blocked|needs-input`, and the domain verdict remains separate. Scratch files are not expected outputs; a standalone specialist outside a pipeline may return its complete body inline or use an orchestrator-supplied scratch destination. When no Work Brief or Plan owns persistence, the orchestrator owns the destination.\\n\\n## Observed gap and evidence\\n\\n#994 already owns systematic output verification, but the 2026-09-24 preflight found no universal producer write/read-back fidelity check or independent judge verification of the final stored references. Adjacent issues cover narrower portions of the contract.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nA task can appear complete while a required state document is missing, stale, duplicated, speculative, or inaccessible to the next actor.\\n\\n## Scope boundaries\\n\\nThis extension owns the end-to-end required-output and report-fidelity contract. #88 retains expected-output completeness, #1857 retains acceptance-criteria validation, #2182 retains worker-report structure, #3106 retains completion-schema work, and #3423 retains transport-status and domain-verdict vocabulary. This extension does not redefine those owners or make production assets out of state outputs.\\n\\n## Readiness and acceptance conditions\\n\\nAcceptance requires observable evidence that every declared output is persisted and addressable by name/reference, the producer has read it back and checked it against what occurred, the judge has independently read and verified it, and non-complete outcomes record absence and reason without placeholders. All DH role reports conform to #3423's separated transport, durable-result, and domain-verdict fields. A completion claim with any required output absent or unverified must be rejected.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"594ce07ad0a1a05b625f0ae2ec38d77e8903c3326f864c9bcd825c51b789783b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABWiraVQ\"}"}